<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>You've decided to calculate the area under the function <m>f(x) = \sin(x - \frac{\pi}{2})</m> between <m>x = 0</m> and <m>x= \pi</m>. Which of the following choices represent this best graphically?</p>
  <answer name="ans1a">
    <choice>
      <graph size="small" xmin="-2pi" xmax="2pi" ymin="-3/2" ymax="3/2" grid="pi/2 1/2" xTickScaleFactor="pi">
        <function name="sinFunc">sin(x)</function>
        <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$sinFunc" boundaryValues="0 pi" styleNumber="2" />
      </graph>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <graph size="small" xmin="-2pi" xmax="2pi" ymin="-1/2" ymax="3" grid="pi/2 1/2" xTickScaleFactor="pi">
        <function name="sinFunc2">sin(x)+pi/2</function>
        <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$sinFunc2" boundaryValues="0 pi" styleNumber="3" />
      </graph>
    </choice>
    <choice credit="1">
      <graph size="small" xmin="-2pi" xmax="2pi" ymin="-3/2" ymax="3/2" grid="pi/2 1/2" xTickScaleFactor="pi">
        <function name="sinFunc3">sin(x-pi/2)</function>
        <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$sinFunc3" boundaryValues="0 pi" styleNumber="4" />
      </graph>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <graph size="small" xmin="-2pi" xmax="2pi" ymin="-3/2" ymax="3/2" grid="pi/2 1/2" xTickScaleFactor="pi">
        <function name="cosFunc">cos(x)</function>
        <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$cosFunc" boundaryValues="0 pi" styleNumber="5" />
      </graph>
    </choice>
  </answer>
  
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Once you've decided which is the correct picture, you set up your problem as follows:</p>
<p>First, you notice that the graph looks just the same as if you were looking at <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m>, but instead evaluating the integral from <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> to <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>:</p>
  <graph size="small" xmin="-2pi" xmax="2pi" ymin="-3/2" ymax="3/2" grid="pi/2 1/2" xTickScaleFactor="pi">
    <function name="shiftedSin">sin(x)</function>
    <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$shiftedSin" boundaryValues="-pi/2 pi/2" />
  </graph>
<p>Since this seems like a much simpler function to integrate, you make this change. If a little bird (or convenient table of integrals) then tells you the antiderivative of <m>\sin(x)</m>, you can construct the following:</p>  
  
  <mdn>
    <mrow>\text{Area under curve}\;\; A \amp= \int_{-\frac{\pi}{2}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}} \sin(x)dx</mrow>
    <mrow> \amp= -\cos(x) \bigg|_{-\frac{\pi}{2}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}} = 0 - 0</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp=0</mrow>
  </mdn>
<p>What theorem was used to proceed from step(1) to step (2) of this computation?</p>
  
<answer name="ans2a">
  <choice>the <term>Indefinite Integral Theorem</term></choice>
    <choice credit="1">the <term>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</term></choice>
    <choice>the <term>Minimum Value Theorem</term></choice>
  </answer><p>Looking back at your picture, this doesn't seem quite right at all... What value have you actually computed here?</p>
  <answer name="ans2b">
    <choice>the inverse of the area under the curve</choice>
    <choice>you just picked the wrong graph to begin with</choice>
    <choice credit="1">the <term>signed area</term> between <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m></choice>
  </answer>

<p>We can fix this problem by considering the absolute value of the function instead, or breaking the area up into smaller intervals. You will have a chance to practice this in the problem set.</p>
</exercise>
</problem>
  
</section>

